 \\begin{abstract}

 This report provides a source-code-level analysis of the\
 \\texttt{sednabcn/LLM-HypatiaX-REPRO} repository, concentrating on\
 the \\texttt{hypatiax/core} and \\texttt{hypatiax/experiments} directories.

 The objective is to reconstruct the methods implemented in the repository,\
 rather than describing HypatiaX only at a conceptual level. The analysis\
 therefore identifies the principal source files, explains their data flow,\
 extracts representative algorithms from the implementation, and evaluates\
 their advantages, limitations and refactoring requirements.

 The implementation is best understood as a family of related\
 LLM--symbolic--neural methods rather than one monolithic algorithm.\
 The common methodological pattern is:

 $$
\text{Natural-language problem}
\rightarrow
\text{LLM symbolic hypothesis}
\rightarrow
\text{formula evaluation}
\rightarrow
\text{parameter calibration}
\rightarrow
\text{neural approximation}
\rightarrow
\text{selection or ensemble}.
$$

 The repository additionally contains pure-LLM baselines, adaptive neural\
 training, symbolic parameter fitting, benchmark suites, ablation experiments,\
 noise sweeps, sample-complexity experiments, extrapolation tests and\
 instability analysis.

 The report identifies several strengths, particularly interpretability,\
 scientific priors, adaptive modelling and explicit extrapolation testing.\
 It also identifies important limitations, including multiple competing\
 hybrid policies, duplicated implementation, dynamic execution of generated\
 code, hard-coded benchmark-specific behaviour and possible training/selection\
 leakage.

 A refactored architecture is proposed in which symbolic discovery,\
 expression validation, parameter fitting, neural training, hybrid selection\
 and evaluation are separated into independent components with a common\
 model interface and explicit provenance.\
 \\end{abstract}

