 \\section{Introduction}\
 % ============================================================

 \\subsection{Purpose}

 The purpose of this report is to analyse how HypatiaX is actually implemented\
 in the reproducibility repository rather than relying only on a high-level\
 description of the method.

 The analysis addresses five questions:

 \\begin{enumerate}\
 \\item What source files implement the method?\
 \\item What does each component do?\
 \\item What algorithm can be extracted from the source code?\
 \\item What are the practical advantages and limitations?\
 \\item How should the implementation be refactored?\
 \\end{enumerate}

 The scope is primarily:

 \\begin{itemize}\
 \\item \\texttt{hypatiax/core};\
 \\item \\texttt{hypatiax/experiments}.\
 \\end{itemize}

 Some experiment scripts depend on symbolic utilities outside those directories.\
 Those dependencies are included when they are required to understand the\
 experimental methodology.

 \\subsection{Central idea}

 The central idea implemented by the repository is to combine two different\
 forms of inductive bias.

 The first is a symbolic/semantic prior:

 $$
\hat{f}_{LLM}(x)
$$

 generated from a natural-language scientific or financial description.

 The second is a flexible numerical approximator:

 $$
\hat{f}_{NN}(x;\phi).
$$

 The hybrid therefore attempts to exploit:

 $$
\boxed{
\text{scientific semantic knowledge}
+
\text{data-driven approximation}
}
$$

 rather than relying exclusively on either symbolic regression or neural\
 networks.

