 % ============================================================\
 \\section{Method Description}\
 % ============================================================

 \\subsection{Method family}

 The implementation contains several closely related methods.

 They can be grouped into:

 \\begin{enumerate}\
 \\item Pure LLM symbolic discovery;\
 \\item DeFi LLM--NN hybrid;\
 \\item General all-domain LLM--NN hybrid;\
 \\item uncertainty-weighted LLM--NN ensemble;\
 \\item adaptive neural baseline.\
 \\end{enumerate}

 Consequently, the term \`\`HypatiaX hybrid'' should always identify which\
 implementation and decision policy is being used.

 \\subsection{Common pipeline}

 The common pipeline can be represented as:

 $$
D
\rightarrow
P(D)
\rightarrow
f_{LLM}
\rightarrow
f_{fit}
\rightarrow
f_{NN}
\rightarrow
H(f_{fit},f_{NN}).
$$

 Here:

 \\begin{itemize}\
 \\item $D$ is the natural-language problem description;\
 \\item $P(D)$ is the generated prompt;\
 \\item $f\_{LLM}$ is the LLM-generated symbolic expression;\
 \\item $f\_{fit}$ is the numerically calibrated symbolic model;\
 \\item $f\_{NN}$ is the neural model;\
 \\item $H$ is the hybrid selection/ensemble operation.\
 \\end{itemize}

 % ============================================================\
 \\section{Pure-LLM Symbolic Method}\
 % ============================================================

 \\subsection{Source}

 The primary implementation is:

 \\begin{center}\
 \\texttt{hypatiax/core/base\_pure\_llm/}\\\
 \\texttt{baseline\_pure\_llm\_defi\_discovery.py}\
 \\end{center}

 \\subsection{Description}

 The Pure LLM baseline sends a natural-language description of a mathematical\
 or scientific relationship to an LLM and requests an explicit symbolic model.

 The output can include:

 \\begin{itemize}\
 \\item symbolic formula;\
 \\item LaTeX representation;\
 \\item executable Python representation;\
 \\item variables;\
 \\item assumptions;\
 \\item explanation.\
 \\end{itemize}

 The resulting formula is evaluated against available observations.

 \\subsection{Algorithm}

 The implementation can be abstracted as:

 \\begin{lstlisting}\[language=Python\]\
 Input:\
 description D\
 variable information V\
 metadata M\
 observations X, y

 if known special case:\
 apply analytical/statistical correction

 construct prompt P(D,V,M)

 response = LLM(P)

 parse response:\
 symbolic\_formula\
 latex\_formula\
 python\_formula\
 explanation

 evaluate python\_formula(X)

 calculate:\
 R2\
 RMSE\
 MAE

 return symbolic result and metrics\
 \\end{lstlisting}

 \\subsection{Analytical enrichment}

 For selected functional forms the implementation can use analytical\
 transformations.

 For example, a Michaelis--Menten relationship:

 $$
v=\frac{V_{\max}S}{K_m+S}
$$

 can be linearised through reciprocal quantities.

 Likewise, nonlinear scientific relationships can be transformed into\
 linear estimation problems before numerical calibration.

 This produces a hybrid within the baseline itself:

 $$
\text{LLM semantic discovery}
+
\text{classical statistical estimation}.
$$

 % ============================================================\
 \\section{DeFi LLM--NN Hybrid}\
 % ============================================================

 \\subsection{Source}

 The principal implementation is:

 \\begin{center}\
 \\texttt{hypatiax/core/generation/hybrid\_defi\_system/}\\\
 \\texttt{hybrid\_system\_nn\_defi\_domain.py}\
 \\end{center}

 \\subsection{Four-stage pipeline}

 The implementation can be reduced to four stages:

 $$
\boxed{
1.\ LLM\ discovery
\rightarrow
2.\ parameter\ fitting
\rightarrow
3.\ NN\ training
\rightarrow
4.\ hybrid\ decision
}
$$

 % ------------------------------------------------------------\
 \\subsection{Stage 1: LLM discovery}

 The LLM proposes:

 $$
f_{LLM}(x;\theta).
$$

 The generated formula is evaluated on the data.

 Its quality is measured using:

 $$
R^2 =
1-
\frac{\sum_i(y_i-\hat y_i)^2}
{\sum_i(y_i-\bar y)^2}.
$$

 % ------------------------------------------------------------\
 \\subsection{Stage 2: symbolic parameter fitting}

 A major feature of the implementation is that the structure suggested by the\
 LLM can be retained while its numerical constants are recalibrated.

 Suppose the LLM proposes:

 $$
f(x)=\frac{80x}{5+x}.
$$

 The constants can be converted into parameters:

 $$
f(x;\theta)=
\frac{\theta_0x}{\theta_1+x}.
$$

 The parameters are then fitted to observations.

 The implementation attempts nonlinear least squares and can fall back to\
 global optimisation.

 If the raw symbolic formula already achieves a sufficiently high score,\
 parameter fitting can be skipped.

 Conceptually:

 $$
f_{LLM}
\rightarrow
f(x;\theta)
\rightarrow
f(x;\hat\theta).
$$

 This separates:

 $$
\text{structural discovery}
$$

 from:

 $$
\text{numerical calibration}.
$$

 % ------------------------------------------------------------\
 \\subsection{Stage 3: neural model}

 The neural component is an MLP.

 Its general structure is:

 $$
\text{Linear}
\rightarrow
\text{LayerNorm}
\rightarrow
\text{SiLU}
\rightarrow
\cdots
\rightarrow
\text{Linear}.
$$

 The architecture adapts to the number of input variables.

 A simplified representation is:

 $$
n_v\le2:
[128,64,32]
$$

 $$
3\le n_v\le4:
[256,128,64,32]
$$

 $$
n_v\ge5:
[512,256,128,64].
$$

 Training includes:

 \\begin{itemize}\
 \\item StandardScaler;\
 \\item AdamW;\
 \\item cosine warm-restart scheduling;\
 \\item MSE loss;\
 \\item gradient clipping;\
 \\item early stopping;\
 \\item best-state restoration.\
 \\end{itemize}

 % ------------------------------------------------------------\
 \\subsection{Stage 4: hybrid decision}

 Let:

 $$
R_L=R^2(f_{fit}),
$$

 and:

 $$
R_N=R^2(f_{NN}).
$$

 The DeFi implementation uses threshold and margin conditions.

 A simplified representation is:

 $$
\text{Decision}=
\begin{cases}
LLM,
&
R_L\ge0.85
\land
R_L-R_N>0.05,
\\
Ensemble,
&
R_L\ge0.5
\land
R_N\ge0.5
\land
|R_L-R_N|\le0.15,
\\
NN,
&
\text{otherwise}.
\end{cases}
$$

 For an ensemble:

 $$
w_L =
\frac{R_L}{R_L+R_N},
\qquad
w_N =
\frac{R_N}{R_L+R_N},
$$

 and:

 $$
\hat y =
w_L\hat y_L+w_N\hat y_N.
$$

 Thus the final method is not necessarily a neural network and not necessarily\
 a symbolic formula. It selects or combines the two.

 % ============================================================\
 \\section{All-Domain LLM--NN Hybrid}\
 % ============================================================

 \\subsection{Source}

 The principal implementation is:

 \\begin{center}\
 \\texttt{hypatiax/core/generation/hybrid\_all\_domains\_llm\_nn/}\\\
 \\texttt{hybrid\_system\_llm\_nn\_all\_domains.py}\
 \\end{center}

 \\subsection{Description}

 This implementation generalises the hybrid concept to scientific and\
 engineering equations.

 It also introduces a different decision policy from the DeFi implementation.

 If the symbolic model is at least as good as the NN on the relevant score,\
 the symbolic model can be selected.

 If the NN is substantially stronger, the implementation can construct an\
 ensemble.

 If symbolic generation fails, the NN becomes a fallback.

 \\subsection{Domain-specific override}

 The implementation contains a \\texttt{force\_llm} mechanism for selected\
 scientific cases.

 Conceptually:

 $$
\texttt{force\_llm=True}
\Rightarrow
\hat f=\hat f_{LLM}.
$$

 This is motivated by domains in which the expected functional structure may\
 be poorly represented by a standard MLP.

 This mechanism is scientifically useful as a domain prior, but it should be\
 reported explicitly in experimental results because it changes the algorithmic\
 decision rule.

 % ============================================================\
 \\section{Un can produce the same deterministiccertainty-Weighted Ensemble}\
 % ============================================================

 \\subsection{Source}

 The implementation is:

 \\begin{center}\
 \\texttt{hypatiax/core/generation/hybrid\_defi\_llm\_nn/}\\\
 \\texttt{hybrid\_ensemble\_system\_defi\_domain.py}\
 \\end{center}

 \\subsection{Method}

 The ensemble uses both predictive quality and residual dispersion.

 Let:

 $$
e_L=y-\hat y_L,
$$

 and:

 $$
e_N=y-\hat y_N.
$$

 Define:

 $$
\sigma_L=\operatorname{std}(e_L),
\qquad
\sigma_N=\operatorname{std}(e_N).
$$

 The raw weights are approximately:

 $$
\tilde w_L=
\frac{\max(R_L,0)}
{\sigma_L+\epsilon},
$$

 $$
\tilde w_N=
\frac{\max(R_N,0)}
{\sigma_N+\epsilon}.
$$

 After normalisation:

 $$
w_L=
\frac{\tilde w_L}
{\tilde w_L+\tilde w_N},
$$

 $$
w_N=
\frac{\tilde w_N}
{\tilde w_L+\tilde w_N}.
$$

 The final prediction is:

 $$
\hat y=
w_L\hat y_L+
w_N\hat y_N.
$$

 \\subsection{Interpretation}

 The intention is reasonable:

 $$
\text{stronger fit}
+
\text{lower residual dispersion}
\Rightarrow
\text{larger weight}.
$$

 However, if the residual dispersion is calculated on the training set, it\
 should not be interpreted as an independent estimate of out-of-sample\
 uncertainty.

 % ============================================================\
 \\section{Adaptive Neural Training}\
 % ============================================================

 \\subsection{Source}

 The central configuration implementation is:

 \\begin{center}\
 \\texttt{hypatiax/core/training/adaptive\_config.py}\
 \\end{center}

 \\subsection{A-priori configuration}

 The implementation attempts to configure the NN from characteristics of the\
 problem rather than using exhaustive hyperparameter search.

 The configuration can depend on:

 \\begin{itemize}\
 \\item number of variables;\
 \\item number of samples;\
 \\item target sign;\
 \\item target range;\
 \\item number of orders of magnitude;\
 \\item pole-like behaviour;\
 \\item positive input columns;\
 \\item difficulty metadata;\
 \\item extrapolation status.\
 \\end{itemize}

 Thus:

 $$
C=f(X,y,M,B),
$$

 where $C$ is the resulting configuration and $B$ represents the available\
 training budget.

 \\subsection{Transformations}

 The configuration may select transformations such as:

 $$
y'=\log|y|
$$

 for suitable one-sided targets.

 Positive inputs may similarly use:

 $$
x_j'=\log x_j.
$$

 The implementation avoids logarithmic target transformation when the target\
 contains both positive and negative values.

 \\subsection{Reproducibility}

 A description-derived seed can be generated using a cryptographic hash:

 $$
s=
\operatorname{SHA256}(D)\bmod 2^{31}.
$$

 This is preferable to uncontrolled random initialisation because the same\
 description can produce the same deterministic NN seed.

 % ============================================================\
 \\section{Experimental Methodology}\
 % ============================================================

 \\subsection{Core-15 ablation}

 The Core-15 experiment compares symbolic and hybrid approaches across a\
 collection of scientific and DeFi equations.

 The benchmark contains examples from:

 \\begin{itemize}\
 \\item Chemistry;\
 \\item Biology;\
 \\item Physics;\
 \\item decentralised-finance mechanisms;\
 \\item financial risk.\
 \\end{itemize}

 Examples include:

 \\begin{itemize}\
 \\item Arrhenius;\
 \\item Henderson--Hasselbalch;\
 \\item Michaelis--Menten;\
 \\item logistic growth;\
 \\item kinetic energy;\
 \\item gravitational force;\
 \\item ideal gas law;\
 \\item impermanent loss;\
 \\item price impact;\
 \\item value at risk;\
 \\item liquidation price;\
 \\item portfolio variance.\
 \\end{itemize}

 % ------------------------------------------------------------\
 \\subsection{Noise}

 A representative noise model is:

 $$
y_{\mathrm{noise}}
=
y+
\epsilon,
$$

 where:

 $$
\epsilon
\sim
\mathcal N
\left(
0,
0.05^2\operatorname{Var}(y)
\right).
$$

 This tests whether symbolic priors remain useful when observations are noisy.

 % ------------------------------------------------------------\
 \\subsection{Train/test splitting}

 A standard interpolation setting uses an 80/20 split.

 However, the repository also contains dedicated extrapolation experiments.

 This distinction is important because:

 $$
\text{interpolation performance}
\neq
\text{extrapolation performance}.
$$

 % ------------------------------------------------------------\
 \\subsection{Extrapolation}

 The extrapolation experiments move test inputs outside the training range.

 The scientific question is:

 $$
x\notin\mathcal X_{\mathrm{train}}
\quad\Rightarrow\quad
?
$$

 A successful symbolic model can potentially extrapolate according to its\
 functional form:

 $$
\hat f(x)\approx f(x).
$$

 A flexible neural approximator may instead behave unpredictably outside the\
 observed region.

 % ------------------------------------------------------------\
 \\subsection{Noise sweep}

 The noise benchmark varies the noise level and observes:

 $$
R^2(\sigma).
$$

 This produces a robustness curve rather than a single performance number.

 % ------------------------------------------------------------\
 \\subsection{Sample complexity}

 The sample-complexity experiment evaluates:

 $$
R^2(N),
$$

 where $N$ is the number of observations.

 This investigates how much data is required by each method.

 % ------------------------------------------------------------\
 \\subsection{Instability}

 Repeated runs are aggregated using:

 $$
\mu_i=
\operatorname{mean}(R_i^2),
$$

 and:

 $$
I_i=
\operatorname{std}(R_i^2).
$$

 A success probability can be defined as:

 $$
p_i=
\frac{
\#\{r:R_{ir}^2>0.9\}
}{
N_i
}.
$$

 Thus the analysis considers not only average accuracy but also variability.

 % ============================================================\
 \\section{Advantages}\
 % ============================================================

 \\subsection{Interpretability}

 The LLM component generates an explicit mathematical relationship:

 $$
\hat y=\hat f(x_1,\ldots,x_p).
$$

 This is more interpretable than a conventional black-box neural network.

 \\subsection{Scientific prior}

 The natural-language description can contain domain information such as:

 \\begin{itemize}\
 \\item physical laws;\
 \\item financial definitions;\
 \\item variable semantics;\
 \\item expected monotonicity;\
 \\item plausible functional families.\
 \\end{itemize}

 The LLM can use these signals before fitting numerical observations.

 \\subsection{Low-data potential}

 If the correct symbolic family is discovered from the description, only a\
 small number of observations may be required to estimate its constants.

 This gives the method a potentially favourable low-data inductive bias.

 \\subsection{Numerical calibration}

 The separation:

 $$
\text{structure}
\rightarrow
\text{parameter fitting}
$$

 is an important strength.

 An LLM does not need to estimate every numerical constant perfectly if the\
 correct mathematical structure is discovered.

 \\subsection{Neural fallback}

 When symbolic discovery fails, the NN can still provide a numerical\
 approximation.

 Therefore the hybrid is more robust to individual component failure than a\
 pure symbolic system.

 \\subsection{Extrapolation evaluation}

 The explicit OOD/extrapolation experiments are valuable because symbolic\
 regression is often motivated by recovery of functional laws rather than\
 interpolation alone.

 \\subsection{Reproducibility engineering}

 The repository contains several mechanisms intended to improve reproducibility:

 \\begin{itemize}\
 \\item deterministic seeds;\
 \\item frozen LLM response support;\
 \\item equation identifiers;\
 \\item fixed experimental configurations;\
 \\item checkpoint restoration;\
 \\item explicit experiment outputs;\
 \\item provenance-oriented result handling.\
 \\end{itemize}

 % ============================================================\
 \\section{Limitations}\
 % ============================================================

 \\subsection{Multiple competing algorithms}

 The repository contains multiple hybrid decision rules.

 For example, the DeFi system uses explicit thresholds and margins, whereas\
 the all-domain system uses a different comparison and includes\
 \\texttt{force\_llm} logic.

 Therefore:

 $$
\text{HypatiaX}
\neq
\text{one uniquely defined selector}.
$$

 A scientific paper must identify the exact implementation variant.

 \\subsection{Potential selection leakage}

 A major methodological concern is the use of fitted-data performance to\
 choose between the symbolic and neural models.

 If:

 $$
R_L=R^2_{\mathrm{train}},
\qquad
R_N=R^2_{\mathrm{train}},
$$

 are used for selection, then the selection mechanism has observed the same\
 data used for fitting.

 The reported result can therefore be optimistic.

 The correct protocol should use:

 $$
D_{\mathrm{train}}
\rightarrow
\text{fit},
$$

 $$
D_{\mathrm{validation}}
\rightarrow
\text{select},
$$

 $$
D_{\mathrm{test}}
\rightarrow
\text{final evaluation}.
$$

 \\subsection{Dynamic execution}

 The LLM can produce executable Python.

 Executing generated source code introduces a security and correctness boundary.

 The preferred architecture is:

 $$
\text{LLM text}
\rightarrow
\text{restricted parser}
\rightarrow
\text{AST}
\rightarrow
\text{validated expression}
\rightarrow
\text{evaluator}.
$$

 The system should not need arbitrary Python execution to evaluate a mathematical\
 formula.

 \\subsection{Hard-coded corrections}

 Some implementations contain explicit special cases for known formulas.

 This is useful engineering but creates a distinction between:

 $$
\text{general algorithm}
$$

 and:

 $$
\text{benchmark-specific prior knowledge}.
$$

 Such corrections must be reported transparently in experimental methodology.

 \\subsection{LLM nondeterminism}

 An LLM API is not equivalent to a locally seeded pseudorandom generator.

 Therefore:

 $$
\text{seed}
\not\Rightarrow
\text{identical LLM response}.
$$

 The frozen-response mechanism is therefore important for exact reproducibility.

 \\subsection{Implementation duplication}

 There is repeated logic across:

 \\begin{itemize}\
 \\item symbolic evaluation;\
 \\item neural construction;\
 \\item neural training;\
 \\item metric calculation;\
 \\item result packaging;\
 \\item LLM parsing.\
 \\end{itemize}

 Duplicated implementations can evolve differently and produce subtle\
 methodological inconsistencies.

 \\subsection{Large experiment scripts}

 Several experiment scripts combine:

 $$
\text{data generation}
+
\text{training}
+
\text{evaluation}
+
\text{statistics}
+
\text{plots}
+
\text{file I/O}.
$$

 This makes testing and maintenance difficult.

 \\subsection{Training-time dependence}

 Some optimisation behaviour has historically depended on time budgets.

 Although the implementation contains reproducibility improvements, hardware\
 and runtime differences can still affect execution.

 % ============================================================\
 \\section{Refactoring}\
 % ============================================================

 \\subsection{Objective}

 The purpose of refactoring should not be to remove the LLM--NN hybrid concept.

 Instead, the goal should be to separate its methodological stages so that\
 each stage can be independently tested and replaced.

 % ------------------------------------------------------------\
 \\subsection{Proposed architecture}

 A cleaner structure is:

 \\begin{lstlisting}\
 hypatiax/\
 |\
 +-- discovery/\
 | +-- llm.py\
 | +-- prompts.py\
 | +-- parser.py\
 | +-- cache.py\
 |\
 +-- symbolic/\
 | +-- expression.py\
 | +-- validator.py\
 | +-- evaluator.py\
 | +-- parameter\_fit.py\
 |\
 +-- models/\
 | +-- neural.py\
 | +-- trainer.py\
 | +-- adaptive\_config.py\
 |\
 +-- hybrid/\
 | +-- selector.py\
 | +-- ensemble.py\
 | +-- policies.py\
 |\
 +-- evaluation/\
 | +-- metrics.py\
 | +-- splits.py\
 | +-- extrapolation.py\
 | +-- instability.py\
 |\
 +-- experiments/\
 | +-- definitions/\
 | +-- runners/\
 | +-- analyses/\
 | +-- reports/\
 |\
 +-- provenance/\
 | +-- schema.py\
 | +-- recorder.py\
 \\end{lstlisting}

 % ------------------------------------------------------------\
 \\subsection{Common model interface}

 Every method should implement:

 \\begin{lstlisting}\[language=Python\]\
 fit(X, y, metadata) -\> FittedModel

 predict(X) -\> y\_hat

 describe() -\> ModelDescription\
 \\end{lstlisting}

 This allows Pure LLM, symbolic, NN and hybrid models to be evaluated through\
 the same experimental interface.

 % ------------------------------------------------------------\
 \\subsection{Typed symbolic representation}

 Instead of generating executable Python, the LLM should generate a restricted\
 expression.

 For example:

 $$
E=
\operatorname{Add}
+
\operatorname{Mul}
+
\operatorname{Pow}
+
\operatorname{Log}
+
\operatorname{Exp}
+
\operatorname{Sin}.
$$

 The pipeline becomes:

 $$
\boxed{
LLM
\rightarrow
Parser
\rightarrow
AST
\rightarrow
Validator
\rightarrow
Evaluator
}
$$

 This removes arbitrary-code execution from the scientific core.

 % ------------------------------------------------------------\
 \\subsection{Separate structure from parameters}

 The symbolic model should explicitly represent:

 $$
f(x;\theta).
$$

 The LLM discovers the structure:

 $$
f(x;\theta).
$$

 Numerical optimisation then estimates:

 $$
\hat\theta=
\arg\min_{\theta}
L(y,f(X;\theta)).
$$

 The final symbolic model becomes:

 $$
\hat f(x)=f(x;\hat\theta).
$$

 This is more robust than modifying Python source strings with regular\
 expressions.

 % ------------------------------------------------------------\
 \\subsection{Centralised selection policies}

 The selection mechanism should become a separate interface:

 \\begin{lstlisting}\[language=Python\]\
 select(\
 symbolic\_result,\
 neural\_result,\
 validation\_data,\
 policy\
 ) -\> HybridDecision\
 \\end{lstlisting}

 Possible policies include:

 \\begin{itemize}\
 \\item \\texttt{SymbolicMarginPolicy};\
 \\item \\texttt{ValidationR2Policy};\
 \\item \\texttt{UncertaintyPolicy};\
 \\item \\texttt{BlendPolicy};\
 \\item \\texttt{AlwaysSymbolicPolicy};\
 \\item \\texttt{AlwaysNNPolicy}.\
 \\end{itemize}

 The experiment configuration should select the policy rather than embedding\
 the policy in each model implementation.

 % ------------------------------------------------------------\
 \\subsection{Strict data separation}

 A refactored experiment should explicitly define:

 $$
D=
D_{train}
\cup
D_{validation}
\cup
D_{test}
\cup
D_{OOD}.
$$

 The roles should be:

 \\begin{center}\
 \\begin{tabular}{ll}\
 \\toprule\
 Dataset & Purpose \\\
 \\midrule\
 Training & Parameter/model fitting \\\
 Validation & Hybrid model selection \\\
 Test & Final interpolation evaluation \\\
 OOD & Extrapolation evaluation \\\
 \\bottomrule\
 \\end{tabular}\
 \\end{center}

 No final test metric should influence the hybrid decision.

 % ------------------------------------------------------------\
 \\subsection{Experiment manifests}

 Large benchmark scripts should be replaced by declarative configurations.

 For example:

 \\begin{lstlisting}\
 experiment:\
 name: core15\_hybrid\_ablation

 data:\
 samples: 200\
 noise: 0.05

 split:\
 train: 0.70\
 validation: 0.15\
 test: 0.15

 methods:

 - pure\_llm
- neural
- hybrid
- pysr

 hybrid:\
 policy: validation\_r2

 evaluation:\
 metrics:\
 \- r2\
 \- rmse\
 \- mae

 extrapolation: true

 seeds:

 - 42
- 99
- 123
- 777
- 2024\
   \\end{lstlisting}

 A generic runner can then execute the same protocol across all equations.

 % ------------------------------------------------------------\
 \\subsection{Immutable provenance}

 Every result should contain enough information to reproduce the run.

 A suggested schema is:

 \\begin{lstlisting}\
 {\
 "experiment\_id": "...",\
 "equation\_id": "...",\
 "method": "...",\
 "model": "...",\
 "dataset\_hash": "...",\
 "prompt\_hash": "...",\
 "response\_hash": "...",\
 "config\_hash": "...",\
 "code\_version": "...",\
 "seed": 42,\
 "metrics": {\
 "r2": "...",\
 "rmse": "...",\
 "mae": "..."\
 }\
 }\
 \\end{lstlisting}

 This produces a provenance chain:

 $$
\text{source}
\rightarrow
\text{configuration}
\rightarrow
\text{execution}
\rightarrow
\text{result}.
$$

 % ============================================================\
 \\section{Refactored HypatiaX Algorithm}\
 % ============================================================

 The complete refactored algorithm can be expressed as follows.

 \\begin{lstlisting}\
 Algorithm: Refactored HypatiaX

 Input:\
 description D\
 train data (Xtr, ytr)\
 validation data (Xv, yv)\
 test data (Xt, yt)\
 OOD data (Xood, yood)\
 metadata M

 1. DISCOVERY
    prompt \<- build\_prompt(D, M)
    response \<- frozen\_or\_recorded\_LLM(prompt)
    expression \<- parse(response)
    validate(expression)
2. SYMBOLIC CALIBRATION
    if expression contains parameters:

   ```
    theta <- optimise(
        expression,
        Xtr,
        ytr
    )
   ```
    symbolic\_model \<- expression(theta)
3. NEURAL TRAINING
    config \<- adaptive\_config(\
    Xtr,\
    ytr,\
    M\
    )
    neural\_model \<- train(\
    Xtr,\
    ytr,\
    config\
    )
4. VALIDATION
    ysv \<- symbolic\_model(Xv)\
    ynv \<- neural\_model(Xv)
    symbolic\_score \<- metrics(yv, ysv)\
    neural\_score \<- metrics(yv, ynv)
5. HYBRID DECISION
    decision \<- policy(\
    symbolic\_score,\
    neural\_score\
    )
    if decision == SYMBOLIC:\
    final\_model \<- symbolic\_model
    elif decision == NEURAL:\
    final\_model \<- neural\_model
    else:\
    final\_model \<- ensemble(\
    symbolic\_model,\
    neural\_model\
    )
6. FINAL TEST
    ytest \<- final\_model(Xt)
    test\_metrics \<- metrics(\
    yt,\
    ytest\
    )
7. OOD EVALUATION
    yood\_hat \<- final\_model(Xood)
    ood\_metrics \<- metrics(\
    yood,\
    yood\_hat\
    )
8. PROVENANCE
    save(\
    prompt,\
    response,\
    expression,\
    configuration,\
    decision,\
    metrics,\
    hashes\
    )

 Output:\
 symbolic expression\
 fitted parameters\
 neural model\
 hybrid decision\
 test metrics\
 OOD metrics\
 provenance\
 \\end{lstlisting}

 % ============================================================\
 \\section{Recommended Evaluation Protocol}\
 % ============================================================

 For a scientifically rigorous evaluation, every equation should follow the\
 same procedure.

 \\begin{enumerate}\
 \\item Define and version the ground-truth equation.\
 \\item Generate the dataset from a fixed random seed.\
 \\item Hash the generated dataset.\
 \\item Split into training, validation, test and OOD regions.\
 \\item Freeze or record the LLM request and response.\
 \\item Parse and validate the symbolic expression.\
 \\item Fit symbolic parameters using training data only.\
 \\item Train the neural model using training data only.\
 \\item Select the final hybrid configuration using validation data.\
 \\item Evaluate once on the untouched test set.\
 \\item Evaluate separately on the OOD/extrapolation set.\
 \\item Repeat the full procedure for predetermined seeds.\
 \\item Report mean and standard deviation.\
 \\item Report the proportion of runs achieving a predefined success level.\
 \\end{enumerate}

 Recommended metrics are:

 $$
R^2,
\qquad
RMSE,
\qquad
MAE.
$$

 For repeated runs:

 $$
\mu(R^2),
\qquad
\sigma(R^2),
\qquad
P(R^2>\tau).
$$

 For extrapolation, results should be reported separately rather than being\
 combined with interpolation performance.

