 % ============================================================\
 \\section{Conclusion}\
 % ============================================================

 The source-code analysis shows that HypatiaX is most accurately described as a\
 family of hybrid symbolic--neural implementations.

 The common scientific idea is:

 $$
\boxed{
\text{LLM symbolic prior}
+
\text{numerical learning}
}
$$

 with numerical calibration providing an intermediate stage:

 $$
\boxed{
\text{LLM structure}
\rightarrow
\text{fitted symbolic model}
}
$$

 and neural approximation providing a complementary model:

 $$
\boxed{
f_{symbolic}
+
f_{NN}
\rightarrow
f_{hybrid}.
}
$$

 The strongest characteristics of the implementation are:

 \\begin{itemize}\
 \\item symbolic interpretability;\
 \\item use of natural-language scientific priors;\
 \\item numerical calibration of symbolic expressions;\
 \\item adaptive neural training;\
 \\item explicit extrapolation experiments;\
 \\item instability and repeated-run analysis;\
 \\item reproducibility-oriented engineering.\
 \\end{itemize}

 The main limitations are:

 \\begin{itemize}\
 \\item multiple competing hybrid policies;\
 \\item duplicated implementation;\
 \\item possible training/selection leakage;\
 \\item dynamic execution of LLM-generated code;\
 \\item benchmark-specific corrections;\
 \\item LLM API nondeterminism;\
 \\item large and tightly coupled experiment scripts.\
 \\end{itemize}

 The recommended refactoring is therefore to preserve the hybrid methodology\
 while separating:

 $$
\boxed{
Discovery
\rightarrow
Validation
\rightarrow
Calibration
\rightarrow
Training
\rightarrow
Selection
\rightarrow
Evaluation
}
$$

 into independently testable modules.

 Most importantly, hybrid selection should be performed using a validation set,\
 while test and extrapolation sets remain untouched until final evaluation.

 This would turn the current collection of partially overlapping experimental\
 implementations into a clearer and more reproducible scientific framework.

